/target

# The Python client's test server is its own cargo workspace, so the root
# `/target` rule does not reach its build output — 2.4k files and a 100 MB
# binary, which GitHub refuses. Same root cause as the rot recorded in
# 2026-09-13-python-grouped-join-and-a-rotted-testserver.md: being outside the
# root workspace means the root's rules do not apply to it.
clients/python/testserver/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Python client
# The TypeScript client compiles its tests beside the package build rather than
# into it, so `dist/` holds exactly what ships. Neither is source.
clients/typescript/dist/
clients/typescript/dist-test/

clients/python/**/__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
clients/python/.pytest_cache/
clients/python/*.egg-info/
clients/python/src/*.egg-info/

# The explorer demo. Each backend builds where it lives, and none of it is
# source: `node_modules` and `dist` are npm's, and `backends/go/go` is a stray
# binary from `go build` with no -o. Same lesson as the test server's `target`:
# a nested build directory escapes a root-anchored rule, so name each one.
examples/explorer/backends/node/node_modules/
examples/explorer/backends/node/dist/

# The deployed example's Go and TypeScript checks, same reasons: npm's tree and
# tsc's output are not source, and `go build -o` writes into a temp dir rather
# than here so there is nothing to ignore on the Go side.
examples/deployed/node/node_modules/
examples/deployed/node/dist/
# And `go build` with no -o writes here, the same stray as the explorer's.
# `run.sh` passes -o into a temp dir; this is for a person who typed the plain
# command while reading the file.
examples/deployed/go/go
examples/explorer/backends/go/go
examples/explorer/web/node_modules/
examples/explorer/web/dist/
examples/explorer/web/dist-test/

# The batch benchmark, same three shapes as the two examples above.
#
# Named after committing all three by accident: 253 files and half a million
# lines of `node_modules` went in with the benchmark itself, because the rules
# above are per-directory and a new directory matches none of them. The
# per-directory style is deliberate — a blanket `**/node_modules/` would also
# hide one that someone genuinely meant to vendor — but the cost is that every
# new example must add its own line, and nothing enforces that. If this happens
# a third time, the answer is a check rather than another comment.
examples/batchbench/node/node_modules/
examples/batchbench/node/dist/
# `go build` with no -o writes the binary here, named for the module.
examples/batchbench/go/batchbench

# Built by site/build-wasm.sh, never committed: a checked-in binary
# drifts from the kernel it claims to be.
site/slate_wasm.js
site/slate_wasm_bg.wasm
